1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,125
A neon sign glows red.

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:04,429
A comb lifts tiny bits of paper.

3
00:00:04,429 --> 00:00:07,727
A spark jumps from your
finger to a door handle.

4
00:00:08,127 --> 00:00:09,806
Three small surprises.

5
00:00:09,806 --> 00:00:12,626
One tiny particle is behind all three.

6
00:00:13,076 --> 00:00:15,143
Diya sees it at her study table.

7
00:00:15,143 --> 00:00:18,476
She combs her hair, and the
comb picks up torn paper.

8
00:00:18,826 --> 00:00:21,174
Something moved from her hair to the comb.

9
00:00:21,174 --> 00:00:23,174
Something much smaller than an atom.

10
00:00:23,674 --> 00:00:27,844
By the end, you will know the three
particles in every atom, and how each

11
00:00:27,844 --> 00:00:28,869
one was caught.

12
00:00:29,519 --> 00:00:31,375
So what is inside an atom?

13
00:00:31,725 --> 00:00:33,611
Three sub-atomic particles.

14
00:00:33,611 --> 00:00:36,293
Sub-atomic means smaller than an atom.

15
00:00:36,643 --> 00:00:39,349
The electron carries a negative charge.

16
00:00:39,349 --> 00:00:41,950
The proton carries a positive charge.

17
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,636
And the neutron carries no charge.

18
00:00:44,636 --> 00:00:45,779
It is neutral.

19
00:00:46,379 --> 00:00:48,331
They are far too small to see.

20
00:00:48,331 --> 00:00:50,867
So scientists caught
them with glass tubes.

21
00:00:51,517 --> 00:00:55,812
On our molecule bench: a glass tube,
with almost all the air pumped out.

22
00:00:56,162 --> 00:00:58,006
Inside, two metal plates.

23
00:00:58,006 --> 00:01:01,034
The negative cathode,
and the positive anode.

24
00:01:01,434 --> 00:01:03,420
Switch on a very high voltage.

25
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:07,741
A ray leaves the cathode and races
to the anode, in a straight line.

26
00:01:08,141 --> 00:01:09,605
The ray is invisible.

27
00:01:09,605 --> 00:01:13,465
But where it hits a zinc sulphide
coating, the coating glows.

28
00:01:13,865 --> 00:01:15,780
Now add two charged plates.

29
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:18,522
The ray bends towards the positive plate.

30
00:01:18,922 --> 00:01:23,979
So the ray is made of negative
particles, the same for any metal or gas.

31
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:25,470
These are electrons.

32
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:29,012
Now make holes in the
cathode, and look behind it.

33
00:01:29,412 --> 00:01:32,811
A second ray streams the
other way, through the holes.

34
00:01:32,811 --> 00:01:35,773
These canal rays bend
towards the negative plate.

35
00:01:35,773 --> 00:01:37,207
So they are positive.

36
00:01:37,607 --> 00:01:40,368
Change the gas, and these rays change too.

37
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:43,705
The lightest of them, from
hydrogen, is the proton.

38
00:01:44,355 --> 00:01:45,457
Side by side.

39
00:01:45,457 --> 00:01:49,072
Cathode rays: negative,
and the same for every gas.

40
00:01:49,072 --> 00:01:52,573
Canal rays: positive, and
different for every gas.

41
00:01:52,973 --> 00:01:54,161
Pause and think.

42
00:01:54,161 --> 00:01:58,292
Why do canal rays change with
the gas, but cathode rays do not?

43
00:02:01,492 --> 00:02:05,008
Because canal rays are gas
atoms that lost electrons.

44
00:02:05,008 --> 00:02:07,522
Each gas leaves a different leftover.

45
00:02:07,522 --> 00:02:10,363
But electrons are the same in every atom.

46
00:02:11,013 --> 00:02:13,143
Now, how heavy is one electron?

47
00:02:13,143 --> 00:02:15,725
Let's work it out, one step at a time.

48
00:02:16,075 --> 00:02:16,975
Step one.

49
00:02:16,961 --> 00:02:21,948
In eighteen ninety seven, JJ Thomson
measured how much electrons bend.

50
00:02:21,948 --> 00:02:27,249
That gave e over m, one point seven
five eight eight times ten to the eleven

51
00:02:27,249 --> 00:02:28,901
coulombs per kilogram.

52
00:02:29,651 --> 00:02:30,590
Step two.

53
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:33,347
Millikan's oil drops gave the charge.

54
00:02:33,347 --> 00:02:38,542
e is one point six zero two times
ten to the minus nineteen coulombs.

55
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:40,404
Step three.

56
00:02:40,404 --> 00:02:43,632
Mass equals charge divided by the ratio.

57
00:02:43,632 --> 00:02:46,823
So m equals e, divided by e over m.

58
00:02:47,573 --> 00:02:48,547
Step four.

59
00:02:48,547 --> 00:02:53,976
m is nine point one zero nine times
ten to the minus thirty one kilograms.

60
00:02:54,726 --> 00:02:56,754
One particle was still missing.

61
00:02:56,754 --> 00:03:00,974
In nineteen thirty two, James Chadwick
fired alpha particles at a thin

62
00:03:00,974 --> 00:03:02,307
sheet of beryllium.

63
00:03:02,707 --> 00:03:06,429
Out came particles that no
electric field could bend.

64
00:03:06,429 --> 00:03:09,816
No charge, and slightly
heavier than a proton.

65
00:03:09,816 --> 00:03:10,716
Neutrons.

66
00:03:11,039 --> 00:03:12,521
All three together.

67
00:03:12,521 --> 00:03:14,561
The electron is very light.

68
00:03:14,561 --> 00:03:19,542
The proton and neutron are each
about one u, the unit of atomic mass.

69
00:03:20,192 --> 00:03:21,211
Remember this.

70
00:03:21,211 --> 00:03:25,610
Every atom, of every element, is
built from the same three particles.

71
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:28,046
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:28,346 --> 00:03:31,483
Millikan measured the
charge on tiny oil drops.

73
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:32,786
Take three drops.

74
00:03:33,286 --> 00:03:38,231
In units of ten to the minus nineteen
coulombs, their charges are minus

75
00:03:38,231 --> 00:03:43,574
three point two zero four, minus four
point eight zero six, and minus eight

76
00:03:43,574 --> 00:03:45,218
point zero one zero.

77
00:03:45,718 --> 00:03:49,302
Divide each one by e,
one point six zero two.

78
00:03:50,052 --> 00:03:51,960
We get two, three and five.

79
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,571
Whole numbers every time.

80
00:03:54,321 --> 00:03:56,634
So charge comes in packets of e.

81
00:03:56,634 --> 00:03:58,835
Never two and a half electrons.

82
00:03:59,485 --> 00:04:00,951
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:04:01,301 --> 00:04:05,703
Some students think a proton and an
electron weigh the same, since their

84
00:04:05,703 --> 00:04:07,501
charges are equal in size.

85
00:04:07,901 --> 00:04:09,398
Equal charge, yes.

86
00:04:09,398 --> 00:04:10,739
Equal mass, no.

87
00:04:10,739 --> 00:04:15,044
A proton is about eighteen hundred
and thirty six times heavier.

88
00:04:15,694 --> 00:04:17,109
Where is this asked?

89
00:04:17,109 --> 00:04:21,306
As one mark questions on who found
which particle, and short numericals

90
00:04:21,306 --> 00:04:22,294
on e and m.

91
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:24,156
Exam level now.

92
00:04:24,156 --> 00:04:29,023
What is the total mass of the electrons
that carry a charge of minus one

93
00:04:29,023 --> 00:04:29,923
coulomb?

94
00:04:29,782 --> 00:04:31,098
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:36,498 --> 00:04:37,927
The answer is B.

96
00:04:37,927 --> 00:04:42,260
Five point six nine times ten
to the minus twelve kilograms.

97
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:48,067
One coulomb divided by e gives six
point two four times ten to the eighteen

98
00:04:48,067 --> 00:04:48,981
electrons.

99
00:04:49,481 --> 00:04:54,858
Multiply by the mass of one electron,
nine point one zero nine times ten

100
00:04:54,858 --> 00:04:57,564
to the minus thirty one kilograms.

101
00:04:58,064 --> 00:05:02,875
That is five point six nine times
ten to the minus twelve kilograms.

102
00:05:02,875 --> 00:05:05,123
A used e over m upside down.

103
00:05:05,123 --> 00:05:06,632
D forgot the mass.

104
00:05:07,232 --> 00:05:08,693
Here is your recap.

105
00:05:08,693 --> 00:05:10,194
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:16,392
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:05:16,792 --> 00:05:17,692
One.

108
00:05:17,315 --> 00:05:20,891
Which ray bends towards the
positive plate, and why?

109
00:05:21,691 --> 00:05:22,591
Two.

110
00:05:22,169 --> 00:05:24,520
Who discovered the neutron, and how?

111
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:26,220
Three.

112
00:05:25,943 --> 00:05:30,039
Why does finding the mass of an
electron need two experiments?

113
00:05:30,939 --> 00:05:35,245
Next time: is an atom a solid
ball, or mostly empty space?

114
00:05:35,245 --> 00:05:39,318
We find out in Thomson,
Rutherford and Bohr atomic models.
